Date: Mon, 13 Dec 1993 18:36:49 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Women Questioning My Ironic Comments

(I'm changing the name of this thread, as the old one, "Brave New
Extropia? No Thanks!," is getting a bit long in the tooth now. The new
title is meant to catch your attention, as all titles should do.)

Though Nancy Lebovitz likens our dialog to a "two-person game of
telephone," I'll nevertheless respond here to her comments. She writes:

(my posting elided here)

> I'm amazed. I'm relatively certain that what you've quoted is
> what I reacted to so strongly (does anyone know of anything
> from the list more like what I thought I'd read?), and it
> looks as though I may have hallucinated a personal attack
> that simply wasn't there. (Though at this point, I'm curious--
> what did you think was being revealed?)

What did I think was being revealed? Interesting you put it that way,
as Kennita Watson asked for just such an explanation 10 days ago. She
took the precise quote I cited this morning and asked me to explain
it. I responded to her thusly:

---begin quote---

Kennita Watson asks me to explain a remark I made:

> Tim May writes:
>    It is interesting and revealing to see that ubermensch Extropians
like
>    ourselves are debating shoes and bath tubs with such vigor.
...
> In any case, I don't know how to take Tim's remark.  Please, Tim,
what
> exactly do you think they (shoe and bathtub "debates") reveal?

Well, I've participated in the "shoes" debate and now in the "bathtub"
debate, though I stayed out of the "mescaline I have known and loved"
discussion.

To put it bluntly, with that caveat in mind, I think the quality of
debate on Extropians is seriously declining. I attribute this to the
loss of key "idea" people like Tom Morrow, Hal Finney, Robin Hanson,
and some others. And (apparently) remaining list members like Perry
...

---end quote---

Thus, apparently (some) women on this List are angered or confused by
my ironic comments. I even stated here in this last point that I had
in fact participated in both the "shoes" debate (fresh from the
"Firesign Theater" performance in Berkeley, I named my thread "Shoes
for Industry" and the shower head/hot tub debate, so I clearly was not
calling for the debate to be banned or eliminated. 

I just thought it deliciously ironic that Jupiter-sized brain
wannabees were debating the merits of hot tubs and sensible shoes.

Perhaps both Kennita and Nancy are unaware of my scrupulous avoidance
of those stupid "smileys" computer geeks so often use. I put them on
the same level as the nerdly practice of vocalizing a normally
nonvocal response (as in "Sigh"). Some computer habits need to be
broken.

The language, verbal and nonverbal, is made the poorer when irony is
abandoned. (And if all irony is obvious at first glance, it is no
irony.) 

> Thanks for making a moderate reply (and for keeping archives),
> Nancy Lebovitz 

And thanks to Nancy for her interesting comments.


--Tim May


