From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: TECH: Newton and "Help needed-fast!"

Jeff Fabijanic writes:

> And in another, maybe not-so-amazing coincidence, my friend Kris and I
> have decided to take the plunge and try our hand at Newton development.
> We have some great ideas, and two companies who are very interested in
..

Good to hear this! At the recent Cypherpunks meeting on Saturday I
showed off my new Newton and there was some interesting discussion of
what could be done with it. I think most folks who see it are
impressed, even if some applications are lacking and others are not
quite up to what they need to be.

(On the other hand, my hit rate on entering words is constantly going
up, between having entered a bunch of special words I use a lot--like
"RSA," "Cypherpunks," Extropians," etc.--into the dictionary and the
training of _it_ and _me_ about how letters are made. Used for short
notes, reminders, data base entries, etc., it is perfectly fine. Used
as a text entry device, it's a disaster. But it's not targeted at
touch typists and writers.)

Morevover, I think the "Newton OS" (called "Newton Intelligence") is
quite elegant, quite intuitive, and is more than powerful enough to
handle much faster processors, larger screens, etc.  In other words, I
think the Newton technology will be in wide use over the next several
years.

Jeff F. and all the others getting in to the market now could be in a
lucrative market.

> Yowza! Better get that biz plan written! (and better go back and review
> my extropians mail with the keywords "startup" and "entrepenuer" ;)

I hope you're helped and not misled by our comments there.

> p.s. My quick-and-dirty advice to any extropians out there considering
> a Newton purchase. Unless you are the kind of person who loves to break
> in bleeding-edge technology, wait a few months. Next spring should see
> some significant advances in Newton technology and the available software
> pool. Word out is that there should be two new models - a clipboard
> "slate" and an integrated cellular-phone Newton. And we can probably expect
> a refinement of the MessagePad form-factor in addition to a lower price tag -
> maybe several hundred less. 

I paid $619 for mine, and I can see the price dropping to around $450,
but not much lower. The construction is very solid, with several
people commenting that it has the solid feel of the old H-P
calculators (as opposed to the flimsy feel of Sharp Wizards and Casio
B.O.S.S.s). 

Of course, future models may have faster CPUs, more memory, better
handwriting recognition, larger display, etc., for the same price or
so.

Me, I figured I could get _this_ generation, use it with the existing
software for what it was intended for, and then 'sit out" the next
generation. The usual game of leapfrog. I figure that a brand new
generation of applications, a new class of possible things software
can do, will arrive in the next year or so. That's when I'll look to
abandoning the current model and moving to the "third generation"
models. 

--Tim May


