The Eo I have seen is currently priced at around $2300 for the "base"
configuration and around $3500 for the "loaded" configuration. And the
handwriting recognition is nevertheless inferior to the Newton's.

Granted, it's a different market niche. But I don't see people buying
many Eos, so it may all be moot. AT&T may well decide to bomb the
price in an attempt to jump-start the market...we'll see.

Apple made the right decision, I think, to go with the form factor it
did, as the size promotes "constant carry." A large "slate" unit can
then come later, as processors get faster and--most importantly--once
the basic product has been accepted. (If people use the existing
Newton, if vertical apps appear, then some customers will want
slate-sized units. But not before. Eo gambled the other
direction...and slow sales are apparently the result.)

>   IMHO, the 640k Newton is severly limited in that applications come on
> PCMCIA cards and must installed in the resident memory instead of on
> a miniharddisk. What happens if you want to use a wireless modem card with
> an application and you don't have any memory left to make it resident?
> My guess is you have to keep swapping cartridges.

No, most applications are being delivered on floppies and are then
transferred via the Newton Connection Kit (for Mac or for Windows).
This makes several things easy:

--backing up the Newton to a Mac or Windows (seamless incremental
backup)

--removal of seldom-used software

--installation of new software transparently

--ftp sites of Newton software 

(for example, I put the entire U.S. Constitution on my Newton,
allowing keyword searches of any term, outline mode, etc. I also put
the periodic table on, a nice RPN calculator, etc.)

As for a mini-hard disk, such things may be coming soon, with H-P
apparently planning a Kittyhawk (their mini disk drive) on a PCMCIAS.
Howewver, I'm not anxious to get this...flash memory is more durable,
less fragile, and the price is already coming way down. The 2 MB flash
cards should hold more apps than I'll be using for a long time, and
the street price is around $200 or less.

I skipped getting the modem altogether....I just had no use for
sending Newton messages on the road. But I agree that the single
PCPCIA slot is a negative. I hear that "multifunction" modem/memory
cards are coming (shades of the "multifunction" cards in the early
years of the IBM PC).

>   The ideal newton application for me would be a symbolic math package
> with stylus input. No way mathematica/maple will fit in 640k. I would
> also like to have a wireless connection but I'd rather not lug around
> loads of cartridges in a carrying case.

Understand of course that the 640K limit is not like the limit DOS
had...expect more memory in future units. 

I use Mathematica and agree it would be nice to have it in a
Newton-like unit. In a few years, I expect a port will happen. But the
lack of it now is not a big deal for me, as when I do MMA stuff, I
expect a color monitor, on-line help files, my copies of the various
manuals and books nearby, and so on.

(Entering simple expressions and evaluating them is a much easier
task, however, and I expect this to arrive much sooner. I seem to
recall that the Prescience folks, who sell "Theorist" and "The
Expressionist," are in fact planning such a product. Expect it fairly
soon.)

--Tim May


